  "shared": {
    "translations": {
      "en": {
        "title": "Changing a value",
        "story": "A desk register is updated before its value is printed. Read both assignments in the program, then predict what the final output statement sees. A variable name refers to its current value at that point in execution, not every value it has ever held. The earlier assignment still explains how the program began, but it does not force the output to keep showing eight. Use the trace to stop between the assignments and distinguish the old value from the current one before running the complete sequence.",
        "idea": "Assignment changes the value associated with a name. In these tracks, the single equals sign in the shown statements performs that binding or replacement; it is not a question asking whether two values are equal. The first statement creates count with value 8. The second assigns 9 to the same name. The third reads count and therefore prints 9. Read the right side as the value being supplied and the left side as the name receiving it. Later lessons introduce comparisons; keep that operation separate from assignment.",
        "make": "Change the second assigned value to 10 while leaving the first assignment and output statement intact. Predict the result before checking. The block exercise has exactly one intended sequence: initialize, update, then print. A program that prints between the first two assignments can run successfully while displaying a different value. When checking an arrangement, point to the most recent assignment before the output statement. That local evidence is more useful than counting how many times a name appears anywhere in the source.",
        "recap": "The current value depends on execution order. Reassigning count replaces the value used by a later read, and the original value is not automatically printed or added to it. A single equals sign performs assignment in these examples. Cover the last line and explain what value it would read at two different points in the program. Then use recall to check the distinction between initialization and a later update. The next lesson considers names whose values are intended to stay fixed during one run."
      },
      "fil": null,
      "ceb": null
    },
    "translationStatus": {
      "en": "reviewed",
      "fil": "draft",
      "ceb": "draft"
    }
  },
